==Practices==
===Symbols===
===Prayer===
Prayer in Sunneism is seen as a collective communion with the gods through a greeting. 

There are two daily prayers, one at dawn — to greet [[Sunne (god)|Sunne]] — and one at dusk — to greet [[Mōna (goddess)|Mōna]]. 

A [[gaderung]] is a place of worship for Sunneists, which serves as a place of prayer and a social center for the community. 

===Marriage===
{{Main|Marriage in Sunneism}}

Sunneists are strictly monogamists and marriage is considered the most sacred act before the gods. According to the [[Book of Life]], a marriage is "a matrimonial alliance, by which a man and a woman comprise between them a community for life, ordered by its natural character for the good of the spouses as well as for the generation and education of children". 

As such, the constitutive element of marriage is the consent of the spouses. Any forced marriage is considered void and an insult to the gods.

From marriage a bond is born between the spouses which, by its nature, is perpetual and exclusive. The consent by which the spouses give each other is sealed by the gods themselves, so that the marriage concluded and consummated between a man and woman can never be dissolved. 

